PROBE A, NEGATIVE and CATEGORICAL (real 36 -> 86, insns 151 -> 153, frame slots shifted 64 -> 76): delete v0/v1/v2 and read viewpt[] directly in both places --
        dx = *(f32*)(buf + 64) - viewpt[0];
        dy = *py - viewpt[1];
        dz = *pz - viewpt[2];
        ...
        *(f32*)(buf + 64) = viewpt[0] + dx;
MWCC did NOT keep the three viewpt loads live across the stores through `buf` (a u8*-derived pointer that may alias the address-taken local array), so the count went count-asymmetric. This is the measurement that proves the v0/v1/v2 temps are required.
PROBE B, NEGATIVE (real 36 -> 70, a new 52(r1) spill appeared): keep the deltas direct and assign the temps afterwards --
        dx = *(f32*)(buf + 64) - viewpt[0];
        v0 = viewpt[0];
        dy = *py - viewpt[1];
        v1 = viewpt[1];
        dz = *pz - viewpt[2];
        v2 = viewpt[2];
PROBE C, PARTIAL (real 36 -> 40): compound assignment, buf value first --
        dx = *(f32*)(buf + 64);
        v0 = viewpt[0];
        dx -= v0;
        (same for dy/*py/v1 and dz/*pz/v2)
All three load PAIRS came into the target's order for the first time, but `-=` produced IN-PLACE subtractions (`fsubs f6,f6,f1`) where the target has a fresh destination (`fsubs f2,f0,f4`).
PROBE D, KEPT (real 36 flat, fuzzy 95.0000 -> 95.1788): a scratch temp restores the three-address form while keeping the buf load textually first --
        t = *(f32*)(buf + 64);
        v0 = viewpt[0];
        dx = t - v0;
(measured, real 36, but pairs 2 and 3 reverted to viewpt-first) and then, FINALLY KEPT, the assignment-in-expression form that gets both properties at once --
        dx = *(f32*)(buf + 64) - (v0 = viewpt[0]);
        dy = *py - (v1 = viewpt[1]);
        dz = *pz - (v2 = viewpt[2]);
This is the committed form: all three pairs load the minuend first as the target does, all three subtractions are three-address, and the `lfs f0,72(r31)` at +0x188 became byte-identical.
